 ### New features ✨
+* Admin GUI/Visu: The icon library now also imports **PNG and JPEG** images — as single files or inside a ZIP — for icons that only exist as a picture (a photo of the car, a device image). Each image is stored as a minimal SVG that embeds it as a base64 `data:` URI on an `<image>` element, sized from the image's own width and height, so every icon is still listed, exported and served as SVG; the file type is detected from the content, not the extension, and an image may be at most 1 MB. The SVG sanitizer (server side and in the Visu) now keeps such embedded raster images — `data:image/png`, `jpeg`, `gif` or `webp` with a base64 payload, and only on `<image>` elements — while `data:image/svg+xml`, `javascript:` and data URIs on any other element are still removed. This also fixes SVGs that embed a bitmap, such as a photo placed in Inkscape, which used to import as an empty icon of about 1 KB because the sanitizer stripped the embedded image.
 * Logic Engine/Admin GUI: Adds a new Logic category **Energy Management** and its first block, **Surplus control** ("HEMS Lite"). The block distributes the energy surplus measured at the grid connection point across any number of consumers by priority — wallboxes, water heaters, heating elements, heat pumps, pool pumps or any other deferrable load — where a hand-built replacement used to take many separate blocks. Consumers are continuously controllable (a 0–100 % setpoint with minimum/maximum, step size and minimum power), simple on/off loads with separate switch-on and switch-off thresholds, or one-shot triggers (e.g. start a washing machine through an API) that fire once per release, with lockout time and a choice of how they are re-armed. The consumer list is sorted by drag and drop and its order *is* the priority — there are no priority numbers, so duplicates are impossible; new consumers are appended at the bottom, and the budget is cut from the bottom up when it shrinks. Every consumer keeps a stable internal ID, so reordering never re-wires an existing connection. The grid connection can be measured as one bidirectional value (positive = import), as two separate positive import/export inputs (netted internally as import − export), or as a feed-in value only (documented limitation: at 0 W the block cannot tell a balance from import, so it holds and then sheds step by step). The power budget keeps already allocated power in the calculation (`used power − grid power − reserve`), minimum run time, minimum off time, switch-on/off delays and hysteresis prevent needless switching, and "output only on change" avoids unnecessary writes. Missing, non-numeric, `NaN` or stale meter values — and a cleared Enable or Plant-OK input — put all outputs into the safe state (0 %, off, no trigger) and are written to the log; after a restart the block always starts safe and never fires a trigger because of a restored state. Per-consumer status and diagnostic outputs (grid power, surplus, budget, allocated, remaining, block status, warning) make every decision traceable. The block runs its own control interval (default 30 s, 5 s to 60 min). Help page: *Blocks: Energy (HEMS)*. https://github.com/abeggled/openbridgeserver/issues/1327
 * Logic Engine/Admin GUI: Every consumer of the **Surplus control** block (HEMS Lite) now has its own boolean **Release** input, so higher-level logic can lock a single consumer temporarily — a wallbox only while a car is plugged in, hot water only within operating hours, a heat pump reporting a fault or interlock — instead of removing it from the configuration or faking its power. A locked consumer is skipped in the distribution as if it were not listed: its allocated or reserved power goes to the released consumers below it, a percent output goes to 0 %, an on/off output to `false`, a trigger does not fire and a running pulse ends, and its status is the new `blocked`, which is distinct from a missing surplus, a waiting time or a fault. The lock acts at once, also between control cycles, and takes precedence over minimum run time, switch-on/off delays, priority and "hold the last outputs". The consumer keeps its position; once released, it takes part again from the next control cycle — never with its earlier setpoint, its minimum off time and switch-on delay apply as usual. An unconnected Release input uses a configurable default (released unless changed), so existing configurations behave unchanged; for a connected input whose value is missing, stale (beyond the hold time) or not a yes/no value, the consumer is locked by default, or optionally falls back to the default, and the fault is logged once. In debug mode, the consumer list in the block's properties shows each consumer's *Released*/*Blocked* state live. https://github.com/abeggled/openbridgeserver/issues/1359
 * Logic Engine/Admin GUI: Added a Binary Statistics function block (`binary_stats`, "Binärstatistik") that counts how many of 2–30 binary inputs are TRUE and FALSE, so questions such as "how many windows are open?" or "at least 2 of 3 smoke detectors" no longer need a chain of compare/formula blocks or a Python script. Inputs and per-input negation work exactly like AND/OR/XOR (negation is applied before counting). The block outputs the number of TRUE and FALSE inputs, the number of evaluated inputs, the TRUE share in percent (one decimal, rounded half up), a strict majority flag, a separate tie flag for an even split and a "k of n" threshold flag (`threshold_count`, 0 = off). Unwired inputs are ignored by default so that three wired inputs out of ten are not reported as seven permanent FALSE values; the `unwired_inputs` setting can instead count them as FALSE like AND/OR. A wired input that delivers no value in a run always counts as FALSE. With no wired input every count and the percentage is 0 and every boolean output false. The block is purely combinational and keeps no state. https://github.com/abeggled/openbridgeserver/issues/1164
